[[!comment format=mdwn
 username="joey"
 subject="""comment 3"""
 date="2026-10-09T16:57:43Z"
 content="""
Previous comment go me thinking, this security hole may not need an
autoenabled special remote at all.

For example, if the attacker knows that I am storing annexed objects in a
private S3 bucket, they can make a git-annex repository with a special
remote for a S3 bucket that contains files they know I want. When I clone
it, I can manually enable that special remote and use it. And then the
attacker strikes, by updating the git-annex branch remote.log so that
the special remote now points to my own private S3 bucket. Causing 
`git-annex sync` to exfiltrate annex objects from it.

This is really a separate security hole, but fixing it comprehensively
across special remotes seems to be needed in order to fix the autoenable
security hole.

One fix is for enabling a special remote to copy all security relevant
parts of the remote config to git config settings, and have the special
remote read those git config settings on use.

It should be possible to fix this without modifying all the special remotes
to do that. git-annex could remember that a special remote was enabled with
a particular special remote config, and supply that config to it, even
after the git-annex branch was updated with a newer config.

To handle legitimate config changes, git-annex could notify the user about
a config change and have them verify it before using it.

How to handle upgrades, where it's not yet recorded which config a special
remote was enabled with? It could default to the first or the last
remote.log config of the special remote. First would be undesirable when
git-annex enableremote has been used in the past to change the config
in some signficant way. Last risks exposing the user of an upgraded
git-annex to this security hole, but only if an attacker has already
updated the git-annex branch.
"""]]
